pliers motivated to move quickly to 1B process to boost RD5 profit margins\"\n  }\n]", "[\n  {\n    \"MarketProvider\": \"Cleveland Research Company\",\n    \"FileName\": \"DRAM Industry Themes (10-25-23).pdf\",\n    \"FileType\": \"pdf\",\n    \"FileUnit\": \"1\",\n    \"Content\": \"## DRAM Industry ThemesImportant disclosures can be found in AppendixOctober 25,2023## ConclusionWe now expect DRAM ASPs likely to see larger than expected increase in C4Q, with potential for double digit increase as early as C1Q24. DDR5 adoption in server and PC ramping as builds shift to next-gen CPUs; DDR5 premiums seen benefiting ASPs. Inventories elevated but improving in DDR4 & NAND - expected to normalize in 1H24; DDR5 appears balanced. End-demand appears to have remained modest, but see increased buy-ahead, supply in check, & content growth across applications.  We hear supplier and hyperscale inventory expected to normalize in 1H24, which should support ASPs.## Themes from our Research- 1. DRAM Demand: Bit demand feedback remains mostly cautious, with our bit shipment forecasts flat to down Y/Y in 2023, and expectations for recovery to +15-20% in 2024.DRAM Supply: Feedback suggests most vendors maintaining production cuts. We hear DRAM cuts take ~4 months to impact the market, suggesting July/Aug Samsung cuts may impact the market more fully in C4Q. We expect 2023 DRAM supply down mid-high single digit Y/Y, with 2024 DRAM supply +3-4% Y/Y - both unchanged vs prior.## 2. DRAM production cuts remain with demand generally expected to recover in 2024:- \\uf0b7 Datacenter: DRAM demand still pressured by AI spend, continue to see hyperscale units down 10-15% Y/Y in 2023, and flat/slight growth in 2024. Demand for 128GB, HBM, & DDR5 appears relatively better vs DDR4 - with 128GB & HBM still very tight - see many hyperscaler ' s buys now a majority DDR5 with DDR4 inventory still elevated.- \\uf0b7 Server: Demand feedback largely consistent with prior work calling for units down 5-10%+ Y/Y, softness largely attributed to IT budget cuts. We now hear hope for a demand recovery in 4Q23 vs prior hopes for an improvement in 2H23. Feedback suggests units likely to return to growth, but difficult to forecast given broad uncertainty.- \\uf0b7 PC: PC feedback similar to prior with TAM down 10-15% Y/Y in 2023, and +4-6% in 2024; would note that PC TAM feedback ranges from flat/down slightly to +10% Y/Y. We do not expect AI PC to be a significant driver to PC demand in 2024. Feedback suggests AI PC could become a tailwind in 2025. Content growth expected to drive bits as well, as customers shift to higher speed SKUs throughout 2024 to future proof for on-device AI. We hear vendors expected 12GB or higher to become more mainstream in notebooks starting in 2H24.- \\uf0b7 Mobile: Mobile demand seen flat to up single digits Y/Y in 2024 - overall feedback a bit more cautious of late after signs of China demand recovery in Aug and Sept. We had heard several Mobile OEMs increasing their DRAM/NAND buys in China in the wake of the Micron ban. We hear China mobile OEMs may still be able to source Micron products to fulfill export demand. 2024 unit feedback appears slightly more cautious - with Huawei expected to negatively impact Apple and OVX shipments. Content feedback was largely constructive, in preparation for on-device AI and aligned with the general shift of new phones to higher-specs.| DRAM Supply/Demand Model   | 2015   | 2016   | 2017   | 2018   | 2019   | 2020   | 2021   | 2022   | 2023e   | 2024e   ||----------------------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|---------|---------|| TOTAL Bit Demand           | 17.8%  | 20.8%  | 23.7%  | 22.4%  | 17.5%  | 16.3%  | 19.4%  | 4.4%   | -1.0%   | 15.6%   || TOTAL Bit Supply           | 19.4%  | 30.8%  | 23.2%  | 20.6%  | 19.8%  | 15.2%  | 18.3%  | 16.2%  | -7.1%   | 3.5%    || GAP: Demand - Supply       | -1.5%  | -10.0% | 0.5%   | 1.8%   | -2.3%  | 1.1%   | 1.1%   | -11.